\"text-align:left;\"> 14 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> ALBANY PARK </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 2 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 0.32 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 0.41 </td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 72 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> 8 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> NEAR NORTH SIDE </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 3 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 0.29 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 0.41 </td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 29 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> 36 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> OAKLAND </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 1 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 0.11 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 0.11 </td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 28 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> 35 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> DOUGLAS </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 1 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 0.10 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 0.10 </td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 33 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> 4 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> LINCOLN SQUARE </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 2 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 0.10 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 0.10 </td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 25 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> 32 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> LOOP </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 1 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 0.06 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 0.06 </td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 31 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> 38 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> GRAND BOULEVARD </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 1 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 0.02 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 0.02 </td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 69 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> 74 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> MOUNT GREENWOOD </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 2 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 0.01 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 0.01 </td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 73 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> 9 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> EDISON PARK </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 1 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 0.00 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 0.00 </td>\n  </tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n`````\n:::\n:::\n\n\nIn Part 5, I used spatial cross-validation to test whether the model could still work outside the areas used for training. I used Community Areas as the grouping unit and applied a Leave-One-Group-Out approach. This step was important because nearby spatial units are often similar to each other, so a random split could overestimate model performance. The goal here was to see whether the model could generalize to other parts of the city. The results showed that model performance varied substantially across Community Areas. Some areas had very small prediction errors, while others had much larger ones, especially in several South and West Side communities such as West Englewood, Greater Grand Crossing, Englewood, and Roseland. This suggests that the model’s ability to transfer across space was uneven. In particular, high-cluster areas were often harder to predict precisely, even if the model could still capture the broader pattern of where reports were concentrated. I think one possible reason is that the pattern in these community areas cannot be fully learned from data in the rest of the city, which makes it harder for the model to generalize across communities.\n\n#### Part 6: Model Evaluation\n- Compare to KDE baseline\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\n# Convert vacant building reports to ppp format\nvacant_ppp <- as.ppp(\n  st_coordinates(vacant_buildings),\n  W = as.owin(st_bbox(chicagoBoundary))\n)\n\n# Calculate KDE with 1km bandwidth\nkde_vacant <- density.ppp(\n  vacant_ppp,\n  sigma = 1000,\n  edge = TRUE\n)\n\n# Convert to terra raster\nkde_raster <- rast(kde_vacant)\n\n# Extract KDE values to fishnet cells\nfishnet <- fishnet %>%\n  mutate(\n    kde_value = terra::extract(\n      kde_raster,\n      vect(fishnet),\n      fun = mean,\n      na.rm = TRUE\n    )[, 2]\n  )\n\ncat(\"KDE baseline calculated successfully.\\n\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\nKDE baseline calculated successfully.\n```\n\n\n:::\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nsummary(fishnet$kde_value)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n     Min.   1st Qu.    Median      Mean   3rd Qu.      Max. \n4.347e-08 7.231e-07 2.392e-06 6.352e-06 9.848e-06 3.910e-05 \n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nggplot() +\n  geom_sf(data = fishnet, aes(fill = kde_value), color = NA) +\n  geom_sf(data = chicagoBoundary, fill = NA, color = \"white\", linewidth = 0.8) +\n  scale_fill_viridis_c(\n    name = \"KDE Value\",\n    option = \"plasma\"\n  ) +\n  labs(\n    title = \"Kernel Density Estimation Baseline\",\n    subtitle = \"Simple spatial smoothing of vacant building reports\"\n  ) +\n  theme_crime()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![]([1;31mlab_4[m_files/figure-html/visualize-kde-1.png){width=768}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\n# Fit final Negative Binomial model on all modeling data\nfinal_model <- glm.nb(\n  countVacant ~ vacant_buildings.nn + dist_to_hotspot +\n    median_income + poverty_rate,\n  data = fishnet_model\n)\n\n# Add model predictions back to fishnet\nfishnet <- fishnet %>%\n  mutate(\n    prediction_nb = predict(final_model, newdata = fishnet_model, type = \"response\")[match(uniqueID, fishnet_model$uniqueID)]\n  )\n\n# Restrict KDE comparison to the same cells used by the final model\neval_ids <- fishnet_model$uniqueID\n\nkde_sum <- fishnet %>%\n  st_drop_geometry() %>%\n  filter(uniqueID %in% eval_ids) %>%\n  summarize(total = sum(kde_value, na.rm = TRUE)) %>%\n  pull(total)\n\ncount_sum <- fishnet %>%\n  st_drop_geometry() %>%\n  filter(uniqueID %in% eval_ids) %>%\n  summarize(total = sum(countVacant, na.rm = TRUE)) %>%\n  pull(total)\n\nfishnet <- fishnet %>%\n  mutate(\n    prediction_kde = if_else(\n      uniqueID %in% eval_ids,\n      (kde_value / kde_sum) * count_sum,\n      NA_real_\n    )\n  )\n```\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\np1 <- ggplot() +\n  geom_sf(data = fishnet, aes(fill = countVacant), color = NA) +\n  scale_fill_viridis_c(name = \"Count\", option = \"plasma\") +\n  labs(title = \"Actual Vacant Building Reports\") +\n  theme_crime()\n\np2 <- ggplot() +\n  geom_sf(data = fishnet, aes(fill = prediction_nb), color = NA) +\n  scale_fill_viridis_c(name = \"Predicted\", option = \"plasma\") +\n  labs(title = \"Model Predictions (NB)\") +\n  theme_crime()\n\np3 <- ggplot() +\n  geom_sf(data = fishnet, aes(fill = prediction_kde), color = NA) +\n  scale_fill_viridis_c(name = \"Predicted\", option = \"plasma\") +\n  labs(title = \"KDE Baseline Predictions\") +\n  theme_crime()\n\np1 + p2 + p3 +\n  plot_annotation(\n    title = \"Actual vs. Predicted Vacant Building Reports\",\n    subtitle = \"Does the regression model outperform the KDE baseline?\"\n  )\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![]([1;31mlab_4[m_files/figure-html/compare-models-1.png){width=1152}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\ncomparison <- fishnet %>%\n  st_drop_geometry() %>%\n  filter(!is.na(prediction_nb), !is.na(prediction_kde)) %>%\n  summarize(\n    model_mae = mean(abs(countVacant - prediction_nb)),\n    model_rmse = sqrt(mean((countVacant - prediction_nb)^2)),\n    kde_mae = mean(abs(countVacant - prediction_kde)),\n    kde_rmse = sqrt(mean((countVacant - prediction_kde)^2))\n  )\n\ncomparison %>%\n  pivot_longer(everything(), names_to = \"metric\", values_to = \"value\") %>%\n  separate(metric, into = c(\"approach\", \"metric\"), sep = \"_\") %>%\n  pivot_wider(names_from = metric, values_from = value) %>%\n  kable(\n    digits = 2,\n    caption = \"Model Performance Comparison\"\n  ) %>%\n  kable_styling(bootstrap_options = c(\"striped\", \"hover\"))\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n`````{=html}\n<table class=\"table table-striped table-hover\" style=\"margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\">\n<caption>Model Performance Comparison</caption>\n <thead>\n  <tr>\n   <th style=\"text-align:left;\"> approach </th>\n   <th style=\"text-align:right;\"> mae </th>\n   <th style=\"text-align:right;\"> rmse </th>\n  </tr>\n </thead>\n<tbody>\n  <tr>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> model </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 6.67 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 12.58 </td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n   <td style=\"text-align:left;\"> kde </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 5.08 </td>\n   <td style=\"text-align:right;\"> 8.71 </td>\n  </tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n`````\n:::\n:::\n\n\nIn Part 6, I compared the final regression model with a simple KDE baseline to see whether the more complex model actually improved prediction. Both maps captured the main concentration of vacant and abandoned building reports, especially in the South and West parts of Chicago. However, the KDE baseline was closer to the actual pattern, and its MAE and RMSE were both lower than those of the regression model. This suggests that for a highly clustered pattern like vacant and abandoned building reports, simple spatial smoothing performed better than the count regression model. This is likely because vacant and abandoned building reports are already strongly spatially clustered, so the KDE baseline can capture that pattern directly through spatial smoothing, while the regression model uses only a limited set of variables and cannot fully capture all of the local variation.\n\n---\n",
